      "wind": "23-28 kt",
      "risk_48h": null,
      "risk_7d": null,
      "next_update": null,
      "raw_text": "(1) THE AREA OF CONVECTION (INVEST 96S) PREVIOUSLY LOCATED NEAR \n2.1S 86.7E IS NOW LOCATED NEAR 2.1S 86.7E, APPROXIMATELY 906 NM \nNORTHEAST OF DIEGO GARCIA. ANIMATED ENHANCED INFRARED SATELLITE \nIMAGERY DEPICTS INVEST 96S EMBEDDED WITHIN A SHARP TROUGHING REGION \nWITH A STRONG WESTERLY WIND BURST ALONG THE EQUATOR JUST TO THE \nNORTH. A 131232Z WSFM 89 GHZ MICROWAVE IMAGE SHOWED SIGNS OF \nSOUTHERLY WINDS BEGINNING TO WRAP INTO THE SYSTEM IN THE WESTERN \nPERIPHERY. ENVIRONMENTAL ANALYSIS INDICATES THAT 96S IS IN A \nMARGINALLY FAVORABLE ENVIRONMENT CHARACTERIZED BY MODERATE POLEWARD \nOUTFLOW ALOFT, MODERATE TO HIGH (20-25 KTS) NORTHEASTERLY SHEAR, AND \nWARM (29-30 C) SEA SURFACE TEMPERATURES. GLOBAL MODELS AGREE ON 96S \nTRACKING GENERALLY EASTWARD, HOWEVER, THEY DISAGREE ON THE AMOUNT OF \nDEVELOPMENT. THE ECMWF DETERMINISTIC MODEL HAS STARTED SHOWING QUICK \nCONSOLIDATION OVER THE NEXT 24 HOURS, WHILE GFS TAKES MUCH LONGER. \nADDITIONALLY, VARIOUS ENSEMBLE MODELS SHOW ONLY A HANDFUL OF MEMBERS \nTHAT REACH TROPICAL CYCLONE STRENGTH. MAXIMUM SUSTAINED SURFACE WINDS \nARE ESTIMATED AT 23 TO 28 KNOTS. MINIMUM SEA LEVEL PRESSURE IS \nESTIMATED TO BE NEAR 1009 MB. THE POTENTIAL FOR THE DEVELOPMENT OF A \nSIGNIFICANT TROPICAL CYCLONE WITHIN THE NEXT 24 HOURS IS LOW.",
